FILE: doc/resizer.md
FEATURE: resizer-docs
DATE: 2026-08-27
PRIOR STATE: did not exist

OLD_SECTION
(no file — doc/README.md listed Resizer among the modules whose guides were "coming next")

NEW_SECTION
A full guide following the structure doc/auto-writer.md established: How it works,
Quick start, The handle (built / your own), Direction and edges, Bounds, Reading the
size, Styling the handle, Configuration table, Lifecycle, Errors, Recipes, Notes and
limits.

REASON
Resizer was the module with the most recently changed behaviour and the least
documentation. After resizer-fixes and resizer-example, two pieces of its API existed
only in changelog entries and an example README: destroy(), and the rule that a handle
grows the element in the direction it faces.

Four sections exist because the module has a sharp edge that a consumer will otherwise
hit and misread as a bug:

  - "Direction and edges" states the growth rule and then immediately separates it
    from position. The module writes a size and never a position, so a left- or
    top-handled element in normal flow resizes correctly but visibly expands the
    opposite way. The guide gives the two flexbox lines that fix it rather than
    leaving the reader to conclude the inversion is broken.
  - "Bounds" leads with 0 meaning *unset*. It is a sentinel, not a bound of zero, and
    a reader who assumes otherwise will write maxWidth:0 expecting a collapsed element
    and get an unbounded one. The interaction with CSS min-width/max-width on the same
    element is stated too, because the browser applies its own on top and the element
    then stops before the module thinks it has.
  - "Styling the handle" covers what resizer.css does and does not provide, the
    hit-area-versus-visible-line split, and the specificity trap: resizer.css is
    injected during initialize(), so it lands after the page's own stylesheet and a
    bare .resizeHandle rule ties and loses. Both limits of the config `styles` route
    are stated — it is global across every Resizer on the page, and it does nothing at
    all when myResizeHandler is set, since nothing is built.
  - "Lifecycle" documents destroy() and what it deliberately does not undo: the inline
    width/height it wrote, and a position:relative added to a parent that had none.

"Notes and limits" records the two capability gaps honestly rather than omitting them:
the module is mouse-only, with no touch or pointer-event support, so handles do not
work on a touchscreen; and a handle is a plain div with no tabindex and no key
handling, so there is no keyboard path to resizing. A workaround is suggested for the
second rather than left implicit.

The default-driven brevity of the sidebar recipe is deliberate: direction defaults to
"x" and position.x to "right", so a right-edge horizontal resizer over a custom handle
is genuinely one config line, and the guide shows that rather than restating defaults.
